        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: Under Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 519-520 and prevailing tort law, conducting a commercial public display of high-powered aerial explosives and fireworks is classified as an abnormally dangerous activity (strict liability). The activity involves a high degree of risk of severe physical harm or fire that cannot be completely eliminated even through the utmost care, and high-grade explosive pyrotechnics are not an activity of common everyday usage. A defendant conducting an abnormally dangerous activity is strictly liable for physical harm resulting from the activity, regardless of the utmost care exercised or the existence of a latent chemical fuse flaw (Option C). Option A is incorrect because strict liability applies regardless of the exercise of all possible care, and latent equipment flaws do not defeat strict liability. Option B is incorrect because large commercial explosive pyrotechnic displays are not a matter of common usage by the general populace. Option D is incorrect because liability is based on strict liability, not the negligence inference of res ipsa loquitur."
