        ans: 0,
        exp: "Rule: Under the felony murder rule, an unlawful killing committed during the perpetration of, or in the IMMEDIATE FLIGHT FROM, an inherently dangerous felony (such as robbery) constitutes felony murder (classified as first-degree murder in most jurisdictions). A felony is not complete for purposes of the felony murder rule until the felons have reached a place of temporary safety. Where felons are engaged in active, uninterrupted flight from the robbery scene pursued by police, they are still within the res gestae of the felony. Causing a fatal vehicular collision during a high-speed police chase following an armed robbery is a direct, foreseeable consequence of the felony, making all participating felons guilty of first-degree felony murder (Option A). Option B is incorrect because felony murder elevates a vehicular homicide committed during flight from an inherently dangerous felony to murder, overriding involuntary manslaughter. Option C is incorrect because immediate flight while being hotly pursued is legally part of the continuous transaction of the robbery. Option D is incorrect because felony murder applies equally to the getaway driver, regardless of whether a weapon was fired."
