        exp: "Rule: Under the common law doctrine of lateral support (Restatement (Second) of Torts §§ 817–819), a landowner is strictly liable for any excavation on his property that removes lateral support from adjoining land in its natural, unimproved state. Furthermore, this duty to maintain lateral support to natural soil is completely NON-DELEGABLE. A property owner who commissions an excavation cannot escape liability for the resulting subsidence of an adjoining owner's natural soil by delegating the excavation to an independent contractor; the landowner remains strictly liable for the collapse (Option B). Option A is incorrect because the non-delegable duty doctrine serves as an established exception to the independent contractor rule. Option C is incorrect because strict liability for the removal of lateral support to land in its natural state requires neither fault nor owner notice. Option D is incorrect because lateral support is an independent property tort doctrine, not an abnormally dangerous activity."
