    {
        id: 24,
        topic: "Criminal Law",
        fp: "Defendant wanted to break into an office building at night to steal computer equipment. Defendant walked into the building during regular business hours at 3:00 PM when the lobby was open to the public, took an elevator to the fourth floor, and hid inside an uncarpeted mechanical boiler room. Defendant remained inside the boiler room until 11:00 PM, well after the building had closed to the public, all exterior doors had been locked, and all employees had departed. At 11:00 PM, Defendant pushed open the closed, unlatched metal door of the boiler room, stepped out into the darkened fourth-floor hallway, and walked toward the corporate accounting suite. However, before Defendant could reach the accounting suite, an armed night security guard rounding the corner spotted Defendant's flashlight and detained him at gunpoint. The jurisdiction adheres to traditional common law criminal definitions.",
        q: "Did Defendant's conduct satisfy the 'breaking' element of common law burglary?",
        opts: [
            "No, because Defendant entered the building during normal business hours through an open public door without the use of physical force.",
            "No, because remaining on commercial property after hours constitutes trespass, but cannot satisfy the element of breaking.",
            "Yes, because opening the closed interior door of the boiler room to emerge into the hallway constituted an interior breaking.",
            "Yes, because entering an open public building with a secret, fraudulent intent to remain after hours constitutes a constructive breaking."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: At common law, burglary requires: (1) breaking, (2) entering, (3) the dwelling house, (4) of another, (5) in the nighttime, (6) with the intent to commit a felony therein. Under common law breaking rules, while entering an open building during public hours is not an initial breaking, a breaking of an inner door fully satisfies the breaking requirement. If an actor enters a building lawfully or through an open door, but once inside, opens a closed interior door (such as a bedroom, closet, or private office door) with felonious intent, he commits an interior breaking that satisfies the breaking element of burglary (Restatement of Criminal Law / common law precedent). Opening the closed door of the boiler room at 11:00 PM to access the hallway and accounting suite was an actual interior breaking (Option C). (Note: While an office building fails the dwelling house requirement at strict common law, the specific call of this question asks exclusively whether Defendant's conduct satisfied the breaking element). Option A is incorrect because an interior breaking satisfies the breaking element even if initial entry was through an open door. Option B is incorrect because opening an interior door is an actual physical breaking, not mere passive remaining. Option D is incorrect because constructive breaking applies to obtaining entry via fraud, threat, or conspiracy, whereas opening the interior door was an actual physical breaking."
    },
    {
        id: 25,
        topic: "Contracts",
        fp: "An entrepreneur desired to open an artisanal bakery. The entrepreneur approached a commercial oven manufacturer to purchase a continuous tunnel oven for $90,000. Because the entrepreneur's commercial enterprise had no operating track record, the manufacturer refused to extend equipment financing without a personal guarantee. The entrepreneur's sister, who owned a 60% controlling equity share in the venture and held a $150,000 convertible debenture secured by the bakery's future fixtures, orally phoned the manufacturer's chief financial officer and stated: 'Approve the financing and ship the oven to my brother's facility. If his bakery defaults on the equipment payments, I will step in and pay the entire balance myself.' In direct reliance on this telephone conversation, the manufacturer delivered and installed the oven. Six months later, the bakery ceased operations and defaulted, leaving an unpaid equipment financing balance of $68,000. When the manufacturer demanded that the sister satisfy the remaining debt, she raised the suretyship provision of the Statute of Frauds.",
        q: "Can the manufacturer enforce the oral guarantee against the sister?",
        opts: [
            "No, because collateral promises to answer for the debt of another must be evidenced by a signed writing.",
            "Yes, because under the 'main purpose' rule, the sister's primary purpose was to protect her own economic investment.",
            "No, because suretyship contracts exceeding $500 are governed strictly by the UCC Article 2 Statute of Frauds.",
            "Yes, but only if the primary debtor is formally adjudicated bankrupt by a court of competent jurisdiction."
        ],
        ans: 1,
        exp: "Rule: Under the common law Statute of Frauds, a collateral promise to answer for the debt, default, or miscarriage of another (suretyship) generally requires a signed writing. However, under the universally recognized 'main purpose' (or leading object) exception, if the promisor's primary objective or leading purpose in undertaking the suretyship is to serve a substantial pecuniary or business interest of her own, the oral promise is treated as original and falls outside the Statute of Frauds. Because the sister owned a 60% controlling equity interest and held a $150,000 debenture in the business, her oral commitment was motivated by advancing her own personal financial stake rather than serving as a mere gratuitous accommodation. The oral guarantee is fully enforceable (Option B). Option A is incorrect because it ignores the main purpose doctrine. Option C is incorrect because suretyship guarantees are governed by common law principles, not UCC § 2-201. Option D is incorrect because formal bankruptcy of the principal debtor is not a condition precedent to enforcing a guarantee outside the Statute of Frauds."
    }
